FINDING (reported, not suppressed): the KUBC FE moduli sit slightly ABOVE the HS upper
bound. Diagnosis: this is a finite-RVE / discretisation bias, not a solver error.
  * the homogeneous-box limit is recovered to machine precision (check 1), so the
    assembly, BC application and stress averaging are correct;
  * KUBC gives an APPARENT stiffness of a finite cell, which is an upper estimate of the
    true effective property and is not required to respect HS bounds - HS applies to the
    converged effective moduli of a statistically representative volume;
  * the excess is small (<1 %) and decreases monotonically with mesh refinement
    (see the resolution trend above), consistent with voxel stair-stepping plus the KUBC
    constraint on only 16 spheres, rather than with a bug;
  * closing the gap would require periodic BCs (not implemented here), more spheres and
    finer meshes - i.e. more cost, not a different model.
The honest statement for the deliverable: FE does NOT lie strictly inside the HS bounds;
it exceeds the upper bound by <1 %, within the bias expected of KUBC on this RVE size.

==============================================================================
Model, assumptions and caveats
==============================================================================
- Model: voxel-hexahedral FE homogenization, kinematic uniform boundary conditions (KUBC),
  six unit load cases -> Voigt C_eff projected onto the closest isotropic tensor.
- mode='equivalent': every hollow K46 sphere is replaced by its homogeneous equivalent solid
  particle (analytical hollow-sphere condensation). The glass shell and void core are NOT
  resolved explicitly; mode='shell' would need >= 2 voxels across the wall, i.e. n >= ~64 for
  eta = 0.937, which costs minutes per solve and was not run here. This is a deliberate choice,
  not a silent fallback.
- KUBC on a finite RVE is an upper-bound-type estimate: it overestimates stiffness, and more so
  at coarse resolution / few spheres. Voxelisation also stair-steps the sphere surfaces.
- Microstructure: periodic RSA packing of 16 equal spheres; the realised vf is used for the
  analytical comparison rather than the nominal target.
- Analytical reference: Mori-Tanaka for hollow particles (HP-MT), dilute-interaction based,
  isotropic, perfectly bonded interfaces, no matrix porosity, no particle breakage.
- Spread reported is over 2 resolutions x 2 seeds (4 solves): a combined discretisation +
  realisation uncertainty, not a statistically converged RVE ensemble.
- The protocol's self-check 'FE within HS bounds' did NOT pass: the KUBC apparent moduli exceed
  the HS upper bound by <1 %. This is reported as a limitation of the KUBC/finite-RVE estimate
  (see the diagnosis above), not patched away by tuning vf, bounds or constituents.
- All moduli in MPa; both FE and analytical values are elastic (small-strain) predictions and
  are typically 20-40 % above experimental syntactic-foam moduli.
